32:35.760 --> 32:42.080
pressure is actually rising the temperature is dropping rapidly and the clouds are moving incredibly fast

32:42.080 --> 32:50.880
okay new data this is new evidence bayes theorem says you don't stick stubbornly to your initial belief

32:50.880 --> 32:58.480
you take the new evidence and mathematically update the weight of your belief you realize it's not gonna rain

32:58.480 --> 33:05.760
it's just a fast-moving cold front so it's the anti-bowl it's not true or false it's a sliding scale of

33:05.760 --> 33:13.360
confidence based on whatever data just walked through the door exactly bayes doesn't demand

33:13.360 --> 33:22.720
certainty in fact in a bayesian framework absolute 100 certainty is actually a trap because it means

33:22.720 --> 33:29.920
you're no longer open to new evidence if you're 100 sure it will rain you will ignore the fact that the

33:29.920 --> 33:37.920
sun just came out so you hold beliefs loosely bayes holds beliefs loosely belief approaches truth

33:37.920 --> 33:45.520
asymptotically meaning it gets closer and closer and closer as evidence accumulates but it never claims

33:45.520 --> 33:52.960
to fully arrive at a final static fixed point that can never be questioned again and people absolutely

33:52.960 --> 34:01.680
hate this the text points out how much human nature craves resolution we want closure we want to run the

34:01.680 --> 34:10.080
experiment declare the absolute truth carve it into stone and go home we crave the termination that

34:10.080 --> 34:18.080
madame guyon's tutors demanded but bayes proves that forcing closure before the evidence permits it

34:18.080 --> 34:25.840
makes the system incredibly brittle mccandle's maroon network is perfectly bayesian it constantly

34:25.840 --> 34:32.800
updates its kinetic map of the plantation's weaknesses based on distributed local observations from

34:32.800 --> 34:40.080
